    "detailedAnswer": "Answer : A strong answer to this behavioral question should be based on\na real experience and follow a clear structure: Situation → Task →\nAction → Result → Learning. Start with enough context to understand the\nproblem, explain exactly what you personally did, and finish with a\nmeasurable or observable outcome.\n\nFor a intermediate / mid-level candidate, focus on ownership, judgment,\ncommunication, and what you learned rather than trying to make the story\nsound perfect. If the situation had a negative outcome, do not hide it;\nexplain what you changed afterward and how that experience improved your\nfuture decisions.\n\nAvoid vague statements such as “we solved it successfully.” Interviewers\nwant to understand your specific contribution, the choices you made, how\nyou communicated, and what happened because of those actions.\n\nExample : Situation: A stakeholder requested a change that conflicted\nwith the current scope or timeline. Task: I needed to protect delivery\nwhile maintaining the relationship. Action: I clarified the underlying\nneed, explained the impact of the requested change, offered\nalternatives, and agreed on a documented priority. Result: We reached a\nworkable decision without surprising the stakeholder later. Learning:\nPushback is more effective when it offers options rather than simply\nsaying no.\n\nInterview Tip : Use a real story, keep the setup concise, spend most of\nthe answer on your actions, and quantify the result when the metric is\ngenuinely available.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing : Ownership, communication, judgment,\nself-awareness, teamwork, adaptability, accountability, and the ability\nto learn from experience.\n\nKey Points to Remember : Situation + Task + Action + Result + Learning.\nEmphasize your contribution, explain trade-offs, and be honest about\nimperfections.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions : What was your specific role? Why did you\nchoose that approach? What was the result? What did you learn? What\nwould you do differently next time?\n\nCommon Mistakes : Using a hypothetical story instead of a real\nexperience; saying “we” so often that your individual contribution is\nunclear; blaming another person; giving too much background; hiding the\nnegative part of the story; failing to explain the result; or ending\nwithout a lesson learned. Never invent metrics or achievements just to\nmake the story stronger.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nSource Evidence : ",
